  "state": {
    "case_id": "35",
    "case_summary": "The patient is a 70-year-old white female with a history of right breast cancer treated approximately twelve years ago. Treatment included a lumpectomy in the upper central portion of the right breast, right axillary lymph node dissection, and postoperative radiation therapy. The cancer was characterized as pT1c, pN0, M0, estrogen receptor positive, and progesterone receptor positive invasive ductal carcinoma. She was on tamoxifen for five years following treatment. During a routine annual follow-up, she reported a new pea-sized palpable event in the inferolateral aspect of her right breast, present for one week. Clinical breast examination revealed a well-healed periareolar, curvilinear scar along the superior aspect of the right breast from the 10 o'clock to 2 o'clock axis, with no palpable events in this region. However, a less than one centimeter palpable nodule was noted in the mid to peripheral portion of the lower outer quadrant of the right breast at the 7 o'clock axis, appearing relatively superficial within the breast tissue.\n\nMammography showed stable, asymmetrical, increased density to the tissue within the upper retroareolar portion of her right breast, consistent with her prior lumpectomy site. However, a new 0.8 × 0.5 cm mammographic density was noted in the inferolateral portion of her right breast. Targeted right breast ultrasound showed a 0.78 × 0.63 × 0.49 cm hypoechoic lesion that was located in the 7 o'clock axis and approximately 4 cm from the nipple, which was associated with acoustic shadowing. A minimally invasive right breast ultrasound-guided 8 gauge Mammotome® vacuum-assisted biopsy was performed. Microscopic evaluation revealed fragments of breast parenchyma in which a bland spindle cell proliferation was present, forming long interlacing fascicles. The cells showed cigar-shaped nuclei with vesicular chromatin and pinpoint nucleoli that were dispersed amidst varying degrees of collagen deposition, ranging from scant to thick bundles. In some fragments, the proliferation could be seen entrapping lobular units. Immunohistochemical stains for cytokeratin were negative.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
